> Docs\LEVEL1.md:34:## In the headset
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:35:
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:36:Revised later on October 21, after the first Level 1 build (WO-L2) was tested: its warp drew a 
mask around the head that stuck as a window, the site was a flat band of colour under a flat sky with white blobs, and 
the view read as a drawing seen through a lens. Decisions K37 to K40; work orders WO-L4 and WO-L5.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:37:
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:38:- You start in your room, in full passthrough, and can see and talk to the others. Nothing is 
drawn but a panel within arm's reach: the day and three buttons, Ray, digging; Marcus, pipe; Dana, backfill.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:39:- Touch a button, or point at it and pull the trigger. The site materializes around you where you 
stand: a wave runs outward from your feet across the ground; just behind its front everything is drawn as glowing wire 
and blueprint lines over the room, and a moment later the surfaces fill in, dirt, gravel, steel, the crews, the 
machines, until the room is gone and the sky closes over you. About 1.5 s. You do not move; the site is built around 
you. Going back runs the same wave inward, and the room returns.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:40:- The site has to look like a site: a textured ground with relief, a real trench with spoil along 
it, bedding and pipe in the bottom, a gravel road with ruts, fences, cones and barriers, the trailer, the crews and 
their machines, a sky with sun and clouds, shadows, and haze toward the horizon. You can look anywhere; nothing frames 
or masks the view.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:41:- In the site you look and listen. Foremen talk from where they stand; when you arrive, the 
foreman greets you or says what is going on. The only button is Back, on your left wrist.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:42:
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:43:## On the PC
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:44:
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:45:Revised later on October 21: the first page worked but did not feel like a game (WO-L3).
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:46:
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:47:The Plan seat is the site office. The Line of Balance is a drafting board, inked day by day as the 
reports come in, with the PM's planned lines dashed under it and a ruler sliding down the days. Beside it is the radio 
console: one card per crew with its foreman, machine and speed, and a two-position switch, Go or Hold, that clunks and 
sends a radio chirp. The PM has a face (LOB-Game's portraits) and a speech bubble when a line plays. A calendar tile 
flips each day with a tick; when the field slows the clock pill says so and the radio hisses, and nothing on the page 
says why. A daily report line types itself at the bottom. At the end the result is stamped on the board: the finish 
against the promise and the idle crew-days per crew, with the actual lines over the plan; Run again keeps the previous 
run faint underneath. Nothing on the page shows the live site, money, or a verdict.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:48:
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:49:## How a run goes
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:50:
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:51:Push, the PM's plan:
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:52:
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:53:1. Plan presses Start. Site warps to Marcus and watches pipe go in; at the excavator, Ray says the 
ground is good and they will make their footage.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:54:2. About Day 4 the wall at 5+20 falls in. Site sees it and hears Marcus get his crew out. Site 
describes it. Rules finds it on the page: a collapse, pipe stops for 2 days. Plan's chart will not show anything until 
tomorrow's report.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:55:3. Dana's backfill started Day 4 right behind pipe. Within a day it reaches Marcus's crew and the 
two foremen argue: 250 ft a day against 180, and whoever wrote the start dates. Both crews stop. Site hears it and 
says so. Plan holds backfill, which ends the argument.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:56:4. When should Plan press Go for backfill? Rules works it out from the page: pipe needs about 17 
days for 3,000 ft plus the 2 days lost, backfill needs 12, so backfill can start well after pipe and still finish 
right behind it. Too early and Dana waits behind Marcus again; too late and the job finishes later.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:57:5. The result: the finish day against Day 18, and the idle crew-days.
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:58:
  Docs\LEVEL1.md:59:Pull, run again on the same world: the team holds backfill before Day 4 and presses Go when pipe 
is far enough ahead. The wall still falls, but Dana is far behind, so there is no argument. Starting backfill early 
barely changes the finish, because pipe sets the pace; mostly it buys idle crew-days and a fight. That is Level 1's 
lesson.
